# Line-ending policy.
#
# ADDED 2026-08-04, after a CRLF `scripts/rollback.sh` failed to parse at all
# on the Linux server:
#
#     scripts/rollback.sh: line 137: syntax error near unexpected token $'do\r'
#
# The repository has no .gitattributes and every blob is stored LF, but the
# working tree on Windows is CRLF. Editing a shell script there and committing
# it produced a file that bash cannot execute — and the two scripts most
# affected are deploy.sh and rollback.sh, i.e. the recovery path you only find
# out about during an incident.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere. Windows
# editors have handled LF fine for years; a shell interpreter does not handle
# CR at all.
#
# NOTE: applying this will show the whole tree as modified the first time
# someone runs `git add --renormalize .`. That is a separate, deliberate commit
# — do not fold it into a feature change, or the diff becomes unreviewable.
# It is also why `git status` on this repo has been slow and noisy.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Executables and anything an interpreter reads: LF is not negotiable.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows-native tooling that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.mmdb binary
*.node binary
